WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.700
>> If you can fit a line,

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.180
you can fit a squiggle.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.700
If you can make me laugh,

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.155
you can make me giggle.

00:00:06.155 --> 00:00:12.080
StatQuest. Hello, I'm Josh Starmer

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.620
and welcome to StatQuest.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.460
Today we're going to

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.480
talk about logistic regression.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.900
This is a technique that can be used for

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.900
traditional statistics as well

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:21.980
as machine learning.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.680
So let's get right to it.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.540
Before we dive into logistic regression,

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.060
let's take a step back and

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.260
review linear regression.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.440
In another StatQuest,

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.780
we talked about linear regression.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.470
We had some data, weight, and size.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.310
Then we fit a line to it.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.780
And with that line, we

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.760
could do a lot of things.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.100
First, we could calculate R^2

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.220
and determine if weight

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.120
and size are correlated.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.220
Large values imply a large effect.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.320
And second, calculate a p-value to

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.140
determine if the R^2 value

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.500
is statistically significant.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.800
And third, we could use

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.100
the line to predict size given weight.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.860
If a new mouse has this weight,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.600
then this is the size

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.780
that we predict from the weight.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.680
Although we didn't mention it at the time,

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.420
using data to predict something falls

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.680
under the category of machine learning.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.580
So plain old linear regression

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.340
is a form of machine learning.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.460
We also talked a little

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.835
bit about multiple regression.

00:01:37.835 --> 00:01:40.060
Now we are trying to predict

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.420
size using weight and blood volume.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.380
Alternatively,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:46.820
we could say that we are trying

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.415
to model size using weight and blood volume.

00:01:50.415 --> 00:01:53.550
Multiple regression did the same things

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.650
that normal regression did.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.855
We calculated R^2,

00:01:58.855 --> 00:02:02.080
and we calculated the p-value

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.390
and we could predict size

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.890
using weight and blood volume.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.590
This makes multiple regression a

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.710
slightly fancier machine learning method.

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.390
We also talked about how we can use

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.690
discrete measurements

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.410
like genotype to predict size.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.070
If you're not familiar with

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.110
the term genotype, don't freak out.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.250
It's no big deal. Just know

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.430
that it refers to different types of mice.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.165
Lastly, we could compare models.

00:02:33.165 --> 00:02:35.440
So on the left side, we've got

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.980
normal regression using

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.260
weight to predict size.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.020
And we can compare those predictions to

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.280
the ones we get from multiple regression.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.580
Where we're using weight and

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.380
blood volume to predict size.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.860
Comparing the simple model to

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.080
the complicated one tells us if we

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.200
need to measure weight and blood volume

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.220
to accurately predict size,

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:00.900
or if we can get away with just weight.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.210
Now that we remember all the cool things

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:06.170
we can do with linear regression,

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:09.130
let's talk about logistic regression.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.950
Logistic regression is similar

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.610
to linear regression except

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.490
logistic regression

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.450
predicts whether something is true or

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.350
false instead of predicting

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.370
something continuous like size.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:29.770
These mice are obese and these mice are not.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:34.150
Also, instead of fitting a line to the data,

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.270
logistic regression fits

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.350
an S shaped logistic function.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:43.230
The curve goes from 0-1,

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.330
and that means that the curve tells you

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:47.930
the probability that a mouse

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.690
is obese based on its weight.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:54.050
If we weighed a very heavy mouse,

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.050
there is a high probability

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.390
that the new mouse is obese.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:02.055
If we weighed an intermediate mouse,

00:04:02.055 --> 00:04:05.020
then there is only a 50% chance

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.190
that the mouse is obese.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:10.820
Lastly, there's only a small probability

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.360
that a light mouse is obese.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.020
Although logistic regression tells

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.460
the probability that a mouse is obese or not,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:21.600
it's usually used for classification.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.620
For example, if the probability

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:27.620
a mouse is obese is greater than 50%,

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:30.380
then we'll classify it as obese.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:34.460
Otherwise, we'll classify it as not obese.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.220
Just like with linear regression,

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.060
we can make simple models.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.600
In this case, we can

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.240
have obesity predicted by

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.460
weight or more complicated models.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.100
In this case, obesity is

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.800
predicted by weight and genotype.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.540
In this case, obesity is predicted

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.500
by weight and genotype and age.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.420
And lastly, obesity is predicted by weight,

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:04.500
genotype, age, and astrological sign.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.180
In other words, just like linear regression,

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.140
logistic regression can work with

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.280
continuous data like weight and

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.460
age and discrete data

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.095
like genotype and astrological sign.

00:05:18.095 --> 00:05:20.570
We can also test to see if

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.070
each variable is useful

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.150
for predicting obesity.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.550
However, unlike normal regression,

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.050
we can't easily compare

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.330
the complicated model to the simple model,

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.310
and we'll talk more about why in a bit.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:37.430
Instead, we just test to see if

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.730
a variable's effect on

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:39.570
the prediction is

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.250
significantly different from zero.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:44.710
If not, it means that

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.850
the variable is not helping the prediction.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:52.190
We use Wald's test to figure this out.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:55.715
We'll talk about that in another StatQuest.

00:05:55.715 --> 00:05:59.190
In this case, the astrological sign

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.530
is totes useless.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.470
That's statistical jargon for not helping.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.930
That means we can save time and

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.730
space in our study by leaving it out.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.530
Logistic regression's ability to

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.870
provide probabilities and classify

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.550
new samples using continuous

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:18.990
and discrete measurements

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.345
makes it a popular machine learning method.

00:06:22.345 --> 00:06:24.860
One big difference between

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.120
linear regression and

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.000
logistic regression is how

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.380
the line is fit to the data.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:33.000
With linear regression, we

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.920
fit the line using least squares.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.020
In other words, we find the line that

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:40.460
minimizes the sum of

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.120
the squares of these residuals.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.680
We also use the residuals to

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:47.540
calculate R^2 and to

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.895
compare simple models to complicated models.

00:06:50.895 --> 00:06:53.390
Logistic regression doesn't have

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:55.510
the same concept of a residual,

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.590
so it can't use least squares,

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.555
and it can't calculate R^2.

00:07:00.555 --> 00:07:02.450
Instead, it uses

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:05.170
something called maximum likelihood.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.330
There's a whole StatQuest

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:08.670
on maximum likelihood,

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.290
so see that for details,

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.050
but in a nutshell, you pick

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:14.670
a probability scaled by

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.030
weight of observing an obese mouse,

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.270
just like this curve.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.290
And you use that to

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:22.810
calculate the likelihood of

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.910
observing a non-obese mouse

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.910
that weighs this much.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.610
And then you calculate the likelihood

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.970
of observing this mouse.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:35.790
And you do that for all of the mice.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.450
And lastly, you multiply

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.350
all of those likelihoods together.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.850
That's the likelihood of

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.610
the data given this line.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.290
Then you shift the line and

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:50.290
calculate a new likelihood of the data.

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:53.430
And then shift the line and calculate

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:57.870
the likelihood again and again.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.350
Finally, the curve with

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:01.650
the maximum value for

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.410
the likelihood is selected.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:09.570
In summary, logistic regression

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:12.270
can be used to classify samples

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.590
and it can use different types

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.090
of data like size

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:20.050
and/or genotype to do that classification.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.930
And it can also be used to assess

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:24.490
what variables are useful

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:26.205
for classifying samples,

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:31.530
i.e, astrological sign is totes useless.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.410
We've made it to the end of

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:36.335
another exciting StatQuest.

00:08:36.335 --> 00:08:37.870
If you like this StatQuest and

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.770
want to see more, please subscribe.

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:41.310
And if you have suggestions

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:42.730
for future StatQuest,

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:44.760
well, put them in the comments below.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.770
Until next time quest on.

